00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.310
Just expanded with more styles

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:23.470
to support your favorite NFL teams.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.380
Please welcome, Erin Andrews!

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:29.182
(lively music)
(audience applauding)

00:00:29.182 --> 00:00:34.180
- [Kelly] (cheers) Hello!
- What's up?

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:35.933
Hi, I'm apart from you.
- [Kelly] Have a seat.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:38.180
I know, we can't like touch or hug.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:39.810
It's weird.
- I know.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.510
- [Kelly] But good for people
that don't like doing that.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.680
I'm just not one of them.
(Erin laughs)

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:43.724
It's great to see you again.

00:00:43.724 --> 00:00:44.870
- Good to see you.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:46.530
- I was remembering when
I was pulling up today.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:47.950
First time I was here, it was like

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:48.783
when you kicked it off.
- [Kelly] I know.

00:00:48.783 --> 00:00:49.764
- [Erin] It was your first season, baby.

00:00:49.764 --> 00:00:50.780
- [Kelly] I know.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:51.613
I loved that you speak

00:00:51.613 --> 00:00:52.446
- Congrats.
- [Kelly] in sports ball.

00:00:52.446 --> 00:00:53.342
- [Erin] Yes.
- It's when you kicked it off.

00:00:53.342 --> 00:00:55.555
- I am gonna teach you somehow, Kel.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:57.070
- [Kelly] I know, what-
- I am, I promise.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.650
- Yeah, I enjoy it, when I'm at the game.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.360
See, I'm very specific.
- Yes.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.140
- If I'm at the game, the energy of it,

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.310
I can get into it and I don't
even know who I'm yelling for,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:06.143
but I'm excited about it.

00:01:06.143 --> 00:01:07.140
- Yeah.
- It's just like a TV.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:08.030
I don't know.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.850
- Well, I love obviously the food.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.410
- Yes.
- I mean the chicken fingers,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:12.260
- hot dogs,
- [Kelly] Hot dogs.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.093
- all of it.
(crosstalking)

00:01:13.093 --> 00:01:14.250
The beers.
- [Kelly] Yes, yes.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:15.240
- When you go to Lambeau,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.576
where the Green Bay Packers play.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:17.500
- [Kelly] Yes.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:20.150
- Freezing and they have like frozen beer.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:21.050
- I think I've been there.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.190
Is that where they have the big green?

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.110
No, no, no.
- Like the big green what?

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.550
- Wait, is that in Boston?

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:27.629
Where is that?
- God, I love you.

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:28.462
- There's a big old green thing.

00:01:28.462 --> 00:01:29.295
- The green monster.
- Green monster.

00:01:29.295 --> 00:01:31.607
- That's where the Boston Red
Sox played different sport

00:01:31.607 --> 00:01:34.813
but it's-
- I played catch in there.

00:01:34.813 --> 00:01:35.646
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:35.646 --> 00:01:37.050
- They also sell insane hotdogs.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.477
They're called Fenway Franks.

00:01:38.477 --> 00:01:39.680
- Dude, I'm gonna tell you,

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.080
I got to sing during the whole series.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:43.610
They ended up breaking their like streak.

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:44.676
It was like, there was some 80-year-

00:01:44.676 --> 00:01:46.168
- Curse, yes.
- [Kelly] Yeah, curse.

00:01:46.168 --> 00:01:47.001
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:47.001 --> 00:01:48.092
And I like to think I was a part of it.

00:01:48.092 --> 00:01:51.550
Imagine that broke it,
(both laughing)

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.450
but it was really inspiring to be there.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:54.283
I will say
- Yeah.

00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:55.240
- that energy was crazy.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:56.265
Who, speaking of inspiration,

00:01:56.265 --> 00:01:58.650
what women inspire you
in sports right now?

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.500
- Well, I mean, I love any of
the women that are coming up.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.080
I kinda like how it's
changed over the years

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.080
and everybody's kind of
adding their own like kind

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:07.659
of flare and kind of like, you know,

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:08.630
- Yeah.
- [Erin] different way

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.330
that they do reporting.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.870
The one girl, I or woman.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:12.870
I grew up loving.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.800
I used to call her my Michael
Jordan of sports broadcasting.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.170
Her name is Melissa Stark.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.790
And she was, when I was in
college or in high school,

00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:21.620
she was kind of like
the one on the sideline.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.090
She was young and she just
really, really held her own.

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:28.290
- So how have you seen sports
help people in this last year?

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.670
I'll tell you how it hasn't held is

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.020
in a sense that people missed it.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.420
Like all my friends,
- [Erin] Yeah.

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:34.840
- literally, I'm the black sheep

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:35.673
like all my friends

00:02:35.673 --> 00:02:38.020
and my sister, everybody,
they love sports.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.360
- Yeah.
- They know everything

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.306
about sports and they were really sad

00:02:42.306 --> 00:02:44.690
that it was like a void for them.

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:46.220
Like not having that to watch.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:48.160
- Yeah, well, it was hard when we were

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.630
in the middle of quarantine
and everything was happening.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:51.960
We didn't even know if we would go back

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.340
to work for the NFL, right?

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.173
But then,
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.262
- obviously, thank goodness the NFL

00:02:55.262 --> 00:02:57.340
and all the networks figured out a way

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:58.650
like you guys are doing with The Voice.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.266
So and here, have it all on

00:03:00.266 --> 00:03:02.695
and try to figure out how
you kind of just regroup.

00:03:02.695 --> 00:03:03.820
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.610
- [Erin] But I think sports has been huge

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.670
because it just gives
people a sense of relief.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.240
Like when you guys couldn't do your show

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.150
or you guys were doing this from home,

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.610
I just felt like it was a
good distraction for people.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:14.443
You know?
- Yeah.

00:03:14.443 --> 00:03:15.533
It felt like some kind of normal was left.

00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:16.400
- [Erin] Yeah.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.233
People needed that

00:03:17.233 --> 00:03:19.260
for a good four hours
of the day to like cheer

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.280
and scream for their team and not cheer

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.158
and scream at the TV

00:03:23.158 --> 00:03:24.157
- Yeah.
- and just be so frustrated

00:03:24.157 --> 00:03:27.090
about what was going on, obviously
with corona and so forth.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.590
So it was really, I thought
it was so important.

00:03:29.590 --> 00:03:31.330
And we've seen examples of that

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:34.610
like when 9/11 happened
and they got baseball going

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.650
and everybody's like, "Should
we play or should we not?"

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:37.560
because of what happened.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.440
And it's like, the country needs it.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.870
The country needs to come
together and feel some excitement

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:42.840
and root for somebody

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:43.673
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:43.673 --> 00:03:44.506
- and kind of get the bad taste out

00:03:44.506 --> 00:03:45.339
of their mouth for a bit

00:03:45.339 --> 00:03:46.172
- Especially, isn't

00:03:46.172 --> 00:03:48.000
that like the classic American sport?

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.450
- Yes, baseball.
- I feel like that-

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.283
Did, do I know that?

00:03:50.283 --> 00:03:52.210
- You do, way to go!
- Thank you.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:53.950
I think I knew something.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:56.564
I think it's 'cause of
Field of Dreams or a movie.

00:03:56.564 --> 00:03:57.397
(Kelly laughs)
- You know more

00:03:57.397 --> 00:03:58.780
than you give yourself credit for.

00:03:58.780 --> 00:03:59.800
- [Kelly] I really don't.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:01.920
But every once in a while,
I do know something.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.910
What was your first reporting
job like out of college?

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.500
- So my first reporting
job, I'm from the South.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.410
I'm from Florida.
- Yeah.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:09.930
- [Erin] And like I knew college football

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:12.150
and my first job was with an NHL team.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.430
I knew nothing about hockey,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:14.550
when I got my first job.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:15.930
I acted like, you know,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:19.200
I knew the part, I read CliffNotes
like hockey for dummies.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:20.033
- Yeah.
- Right

00:04:20.033 --> 00:04:20.902
before my first job interview

00:04:20.902 --> 00:04:22.310
- Yeah.
- to try to wow them.

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:23.510
And they knew I was full of it.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:25.137
And I didn't know what I was
talking about, but I was like,

00:04:25.137 --> 00:04:26.360
"I'm gonna work so hard.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.193
You're gonna love me."

00:04:27.193 --> 00:04:29.263
- [Kelly] Yeah, I love that.
- Girl, look at it,

00:04:29.263 --> 00:04:30.610
look at us now.
- You can't know everything.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:31.700
- No.
- But to have somebody

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.720
that's willing to like
really do their homework

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:34.660
and really get in there.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:35.670
- I was horrible though.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:36.503
I was bad.

00:04:36.503 --> 00:04:37.336
Like you look back now and stuff.

00:04:37.336 --> 00:04:39.305
- We all were great when we
started at what we're doing.

00:04:39.305 --> 00:04:40.400
- Yeah, right.
- [Kelly] (laughs) Like,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.140
let's all point that out.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:45.600
So your dad inspired
you to get into sports.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:46.670
Does he give you advice?

00:04:46.670 --> 00:04:47.570
Like while you're on TV?

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:49.347
'Cause my mom will literally be like,

00:04:49.347 --> 00:04:51.403
"Why did you turn your
chair for that person?"

00:04:51.403 --> 00:04:52.930
(Kelly laughs)
- Yeah.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.310
- And she's very bold with
like her comments to me.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:56.143
And it's awesome.

00:04:56.143 --> 00:04:57.390
But does your dad do that?

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.622
- Yeah, so we're live.

00:04:58.622 --> 00:05:00.073
So my dad's watching along

00:05:00.073 --> 00:05:01.130
as you know,
- Oh.

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:01.963
- the game is.

00:05:01.963 --> 00:05:03.390
So he's like texting me during the game

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:04.857
and some people are are always saying,

00:05:04.857 --> 00:05:05.870
"You're always on your phone."

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:06.780
I'm like, "Yeah."

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:08.790
'Cause my dad's texting me
during like, "There you go.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.710
Yeah, my dad is texting
me during the game."

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.370
He is an investigative reporter.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:13.890
He just retired.
- [Kelly] Awh.

00:05:13.890 --> 00:05:15.880
- [Erin] I hope he gets to
enjoy the retirement soon

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.740
and get out of this misery

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.950
that everybody's been dealing with.

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:19.890
But he does.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:20.791
He watches the game.

00:05:20.791 --> 00:05:22.580
He'll always say, "Slow down.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.710
You're talking too fast, put
the mic closer to your face."

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:26.840
But then I start to panic,

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:28.670
like if I have a losing interview

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:31.010
or if I know I have a big,
you know, interview coming up,

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.700
he'll be like, "Ask him
this, ask him this."

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.130
- I always feel like Carson on The Voice

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:35.650
like compared to speaking,
I always feel bad for him.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:36.483
Like,
- [Erin] Yeah.

00:05:36.483 --> 00:05:37.316
- "Oh, my God, what about
the people that walk up

00:05:37.316 --> 00:05:38.149
and they don't make it."

00:05:38.149 --> 00:05:38.982
Like you've just sit there and you're

00:05:38.982 --> 00:05:39.990
with their families like-

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:41.630
- And ask them the questions.
- That's hard.

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:42.490
- Yeah, it is hard.
- Yeah.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:44.010
- And the hardest one that I always have

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.294
to do is the Super Bowl.

00:05:45.294 --> 00:05:48.400
For some reason, I always
choose the losing coach.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.210
And you have to interview the losing coach

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:51.290
at the end of the Super Bowl.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:52.937
This is the biggest game of their life.

00:05:52.937 --> 00:05:54.660
(Kelly blows air)
And these poor guys.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:57.540
I mean, I get so sensitive
when I see them walk in

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.210
and they just are like, "Hi, Erin."

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:00.220
And I'm like,

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:01.347
and my dad always texts me,

00:06:01.347 --> 00:06:02.180
"Don't look sad.

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:04.080
Don't look sad and stop talking soft.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:05.280
You have to be like direct."

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.113
And I'm like,

00:06:06.113 --> 00:06:08.937
"Dad, they've just lost the
biggest game of their life."

00:06:08.937 --> 00:06:09.770
"How did that go?"

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:11.730
- But what they probably don't
want is like a pity thing.

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:12.563
They probably don't,
- [Erin] I know.

00:06:12.563 --> 00:06:13.396
- you know, that's why.
- [Erin] You're right.

00:06:13.396 --> 00:06:14.229
- Yeah, they probably-
- [Erin] Yeah.

00:06:14.229 --> 00:06:15.062
- Maybe that's why.

00:06:15.062 --> 00:06:15.930
I don't know, that's a hard,

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:16.763
I wouldn't (laughs) have done it.

00:06:16.763 --> 00:06:17.710
- It's real hard.
- [Kelly] Pass on that.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.013
- It's Carson times 20.
- [Kelly] Yeah. (laughs)

00:06:20.013 --> 00:06:21.140
That is horrible.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.080
Well, you're an ambassador for Tubi

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.140
and you've expanded
your clothing line wear,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:26.820
which is super cute y'all.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:27.820
- Thank you.
- And so can you tell us a bit

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:28.653
about both of those?

00:06:28.653 --> 00:06:31.180
- Yeah, so Tubi is FOX's
new streaming service,

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.030
which is really great

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:35.680
because they have over 30,000
movies and TV shows and look,

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.300
what did we all do during quarantine?

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:38.133
- Girl.
- Yeah,

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:39.580
we watched the streaming services.

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:40.650
And also like,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.990
would they like to call
themselves the comfort food

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.040
of streaming services?

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.240
So it's like old movies
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.340
- that you forgot you loved so much.

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:49.368
Like the other day, Planes,
Trains and Automobiles.

00:06:49.368 --> 00:06:50.620
Did you ever watch it?
- [Kelly] Oh, my gosh!

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.698
Yeah, what?
- Those aren't pillows.

00:06:52.698 --> 00:06:55.440
(Kelly laughs)
John, Kenny, Angel.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:56.320
- Oh, my God, I love you.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:57.648
- [Kelly] You're (laughs)
just, that's amazing.

00:06:57.648 --> 00:06:59.565
Well, we're gonna play a game

00:06:59.565 --> 00:07:02.680
with flying sports balls next.

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:03.513
Have you?

00:07:03.513 --> 00:07:04.346
That's a good tease.

00:07:04.346 --> 00:07:06.599
Have you ever been hit by a sports ball?

00:07:06.599 --> 00:07:07.432
- I have.

00:07:07.432 --> 00:07:09.760
I have been hit at a Major
League Baseball game.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:10.930
I'll set the scene for you.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:12.960
I was on third base side, so that's right

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:15.680
where third base is,
first, second, third home.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.834
Third base side, guy hit a line drive,

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:19.670
which means straight across.

00:07:19.670 --> 00:07:23.270
There was nothing that broke
like where the ball was coming.

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.530
The security guard that was

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:27.304
with me actually ducked,
when it came my way

00:07:27.304 --> 00:07:29.210
and I went like this

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:32.030
and it smacked me clear
as day in the mouth.

00:07:32.030 --> 00:07:32.863
There it is.

00:07:32.863 --> 00:07:35.010
Hot.
- [Kelly] Holy moly.

